  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# 3.05 — Gaussian and Mean Curvature\n\nThe shape operator has two eigenvalues, and there are two natural symmetric functions of them:\n\n$$K = \\kappa_1\\kappa_2 = \\det S \\qquad\\text{(Gaussian)}, \\qquad H = \\tfrac12(\\kappa_1+\\kappa_2) = \\tfrac12\\operatorname{tr}S \\qquad\\text{(mean)}.$$\n\nThey behave completely differently, and the difference is the point of this notebook.\n\n| | $K$ | $H$ |\n|---|---|---|\n| under reversal of the normal | unchanged | changes sign |\n| geometric role | classifies the local **shape** | governs **area** |\n| intrinsic? | **yes** (3.06) | no |\n| vanishing means | flat in one direction at least | **minimal surface** |\n| appears in | Gauss–Bonnet (3.08) | soap films, Plateau's problem |\n\n$K$ tells you what the surface looks like near a point:\n\n| Sign of $K$ | Name | Local picture |\n|---|---|---|\n| $K > 0$ | **elliptic** | bowl — the surface stays on one side of its tangent plane |\n| $K < 0$ | **hyperbolic** | saddle — the surface crosses its tangent plane |\n| $K = 0$, one $\\kappa_i \\ne 0$ | **parabolic** | cylinder-like — flat in one direction |\n| $\\kappa_1 = \\kappa_2 = 0$ | **planar** | flat to second order |\n| $\\kappa_1 = \\kappa_2 \\ne 0$ | **umbilic** | curving equally in all directions |\n\n$H$ tells you something else entirely: it is the gradient of area. A surface with $H = 0$ everywhere is a critical point of the area functional — a **minimal surface**, the shape a soap film takes.\n\nThe notebook works through surfaces of revolution, where a single formula covers everything, and then meets the **pseudosphere**: a surface of constant $K = -1$, which turns out to be a physical model of the hyperbolic plane of 2.06.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncross3(a,b) := [a[2]*b[3]-a[3]*b[2], a[3]*b[1]-a[1]*b[3], a[1]*b[2]-a[2]*b[1]]$\npd(s,var)   := map(lambda([e], diff(e, var)), s)$\nts(e)       := trigsimp(e)$\n\nFFF(s,u_,v_) := block([su,sv], su : pd(s,u_), sv : pd(s,v_),\n  matrix([ts(su.su), ts(su.sv)], [ts(su.sv), ts(sv.sv)]))$\nunitnormal(s,u_,v_) := block([n], n : cross3(pd(s,u_), pd(s,v_)),\n  map(ts, n/sqrt(ts(n . n))))$\nSFF(s,u_,v_) := block([nn,suu,suv,svv],\n  nn : unitnormal(s,u_,v_),\n  suu : pd(pd(s,u_),u_), suv : pd(pd(s,u_),v_), svv : pd(pd(s,v_),v_),\n  matrix([ts(suu.nn), ts(suv.nn)], [ts(suv.nn), ts(svv.nn)]))$\nKof(s,u_,v_) := ts( determinant(SFF(s,u_,v_))/determinant(FFF(s,u_,v_)) )$\nHof(s,u_,v_) := block([I1,I2],\n  I1 : FFF(s,u_,v_), I2 : SFF(s,u_,v_),\n  ts( (I1[1][1]*I2[2][2] - 2*I1[1][2]*I2[1][2] + I1[2][2]*I2[1][1])\n      /(2*determinant(I1)) ) )$\n\n/* --- Surfaces of revolution: profile (f(u), g(u)) spun about the z-axis --- */\nsurfrev(f_, g_) := [f_*cos(v), f_*sin(v), g_]$\n\ndepends([f,g], u)$\nsrev  : surfrev(f, g)$\nK_gen : ratsimp( Kof(srev, u, v) )$\nprint(\"surface of revolution, general profile (f(u), g(u)):\")$\nprint(\"   K = \", K_gen)$\n\n/* A unit-speed profile satisfies f'² + g'² = 1.  Differentiating that gives\n   f'f'' + g'g'' = 0, so g'g'' = −f'f''.  Both relations are needed. */\nK1 : ratsubst(1, diff(f,u)^2 + diff(g,u)^2, K_gen)$\nK2 : ratsubst(-diff(f,u)*diff(f,u,2), diff(g,u)*diff(g,u,2), K1)$\nK3 : ratsimp( ratsubst(1 - diff(f,u)^2, diff(g,u)^2, K2) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"with a unit-speed profile:\")$\nprint(\"   after f'² + g'² = 1        : \", K1)$\nprint(\"   after g'g'' = −f'f''       : \", ratsimp(K2))$\nprint(\"   after g'² = 1 − f'²        : \", K3)$\nprint(\"   — the classical result K = −f''/f ✓\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 7,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "surface of revolution, general profile (f(u), g(u)):\n   K =\n       ('diff(f,u,1)*'diff(g,u,1)*'diff(g,u,2)-'diff(f,u,2)*('diff(g,u,1))^2)\n        /(f*('diff(g,u,1))^4+2*f*('diff(f,u,1))^2*('diff(g,u,1))^2\n                            +f*('diff(f,u,1))^4)\nwith a unit-speed profile:\n   after f'² + g'² = 1        :\n                                ('diff(f,u,1)*'diff(g,u,1)*'diff(g,u,2)\n                                 +(('diff(f,u,1))^2-1)*'diff(f,u,2))\n                                 /f\n   after g'g'' = −f'f''       :  -('diff(f,u,2)/f)\n   after g'² = 1 − f'²        :  -('diff(f,u,2)/f)\n   — the classical result K = −f''/f ✓"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- A gallery, classified by the sign of K --- */\nclassify(Kv) := if Kv = 0 then \"parabolic or planar\"\n                elseif Kv > 0 then \"elliptic (bowl)\"\n                else \"hyperbolic (saddle)\"$\n\nassume(R > 0)$\nsphere2  : [R*sin(u)*cos(v), R*sin(u)*sin(v), R*cos(u)]$\ncyl2     : [R*cos(u), R*sin(u), v]$\ncatenoid : [cosh(u)*cos(v), cosh(u)*sin(v), u]$\nhelicoid : [u*cos(v), u*sin(v), v]$\nparaboloid : [u, v, u^2 + v^2]$\nsaddle2  : [u, v, u^2 - v^2]$\n\nprint(\"   surface        K                          H\")$\nfor su in [[\"sphere    \", sphere2], [\"cylinder  \", cyl2],\n           [\"paraboloid\", paraboloid], [\"saddle    \", saddle2],\n           [\"catenoid  \", catenoid], [\"helicoid  \", helicoid]] do\n  print(\"   \", su[1], \"  \", Kof(su[2],u,v), \"        \", Hof(su[2],u,v))$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"at the origin, the paraboloid and the saddle:\")$\nprint(\"   paraboloid: K = \", subst([u=0,v=0], Kof(paraboloid,u,v)),\n      \"  → \", classify(subst([u=0,v=0], Kof(paraboloid,u,v))))$\nprint(\"   saddle    : K = \", subst([u=0,v=0], Kof(saddle2,u,v)),\n      \"  → \", classify(subst([u=0,v=0], Kof(saddle2,u,v))))$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"the catenoid and the helicoid both have H = 0 identically:\")$\nprint(\"   catenoid H = \", Hof(catenoid,u,v), \"     helicoid H = \", Hof(helicoid,u,v))$\nprint(\"   these are MINIMAL surfaces — soap films (see 3.09)\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 8,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "   surface        K                          H\n    sphere        1/R^2          -(abs(sin(u))/(R*sin(u)))\n    cylinder      0          -(1/(2*R))\n    paraboloid    4/(16*v^4+(32*u^2+8)*v^2+16*u^4+8*u^2+1)\n   (sqrt(4*v^2+4*u^2+1)*(4*v^2+4*u^2+2))\n    /(16*v^4+(32*u^2+8)*v^2+16*u^4+8*u^2+1)\n    saddle        -(4/(16*v^4+(32*u^2+8)*v^2+16*u^4+8*u^2+1))\n   ((4*v^2-4*u^2)*sqrt(4*v^2+4*u^2+1))/(16*v^4+(32*u^2+8)*v^2+16*u^4+8*u^2+1)\n    catenoid      -(1/cosh(u)^4)          0\n    helicoid      -(1/(u^4+2*u^2+1))          0\nat the origin, the paraboloid and the saddle:\n   paraboloid: K =  4   →  elliptic (bowl)\n   saddle    : K =  -4   →  hyperbolic (saddle)\nthe catenoid and the helicoid both have H = 0 identically:\n   catenoid H =  0      helicoid H =  0\n   these are MINIMAL surfaces — soap films (see 3.09)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## The pseudosphere: hyperbolic geometry made of metal\n\nAmong surfaces of revolution, ask for **constant** curvature. With a unit-speed profile the condition $K = -1$ becomes an ODE:\n\n$$-\\frac{f''}{f} = -1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad f'' = f,$$\n\nwhose solutions are combinations of $e^{u}$ and $e^{-u}$. Taking $f(u) = e^{-u}$ and integrating $g' = \\sqrt{1 - f'^2}$ produces the **tractricoid** or **pseudosphere** — the surface swept by rotating a *tractrix* about its asymptote.\n\nIn the standard parametrisation,\n\n$$\\sigma(u,v) = \\bigl(\\operatorname{sech} u\\,\\cos v,\\;\\operatorname{sech} u\\,\\sin v,\\; u - \\tanh u\\bigr),$$\n\nand the claim is $K \\equiv -1$ everywhere.\n\n**Why this matters.** In 2.06 the hyperbolic plane was built abstractly — a half-plane with an invented metric $|dz|/\\operatorname{Im}z$, justified because $PSL_2(\\mathbb R)$ preserved it. The pseudosphere is a surface *sitting in ordinary space* with that same geometry: it is locally isometric to the hyperbolic plane, and Beltrami used exactly this in 1868 to argue that non-Euclidean geometry was as consistent as Euclidean geometry, since a model of it could be built inside Euclidean space.\n\n**Why it is only a partial model.** The pseudosphere is not the whole hyperbolic plane. It has a singular edge at $u = 0$, it is incomplete, and it represents only a portion of $\\mathbb H$. Hilbert proved in 1901 that this is unavoidable: **no complete surface of constant negative curvature can be embedded in $\\mathbb R^3$**. The hyperbolic plane genuinely does not fit into ordinary space — which is why 2.06 had to construct it abstractly rather than draw it.\n\nCompare the positive case, where there is no such obstruction: the sphere of radius $R$ has $K = 1/R^2 > 0$ constant, is complete, and sits happily in $\\mathbb R^3$. The asymmetry between $K > 0$ and $K < 0$ here is real, not an artefact of our constructions.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- The pseudosphere (tractricoid) --- */\nassume(u > 0)$\npseudo : [ sech(u)*cos(v), sech(u)*sin(v), u - tanh(u) ]$\n\nKp : ratsimp( Kof(pseudo, u, v) )$\nprint(\"pseudosphere σ = (sech u cos v, sech u sin v, u − tanh u):\")$\nprint(\"   I = \", FFF(pseudo,u,v))$\nprint(\"   K = \", Kp)$\n\n/* Collapse it using cosh² − 1 = sinh² and sinh² + 1 = cosh². */\nKp2 : ratsimp( ratsubst(sinh(u)^2, cosh(u)^2 - 1, Kp) )$\nKp3 : ratsimp( ratsubst(cosh(u)^2, sinh(u)^2 + 1, Kp2) )$\nprint(\"   after cosh²−1 = sinh² : \", Kp2)$\nprint(\"   after sinh²+1 = cosh² : \", Kp3, \"   — constant, K ≡ −1 ✓\")$\n\n/* Numerical confirmation at several points */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   u        K(u)\")$\nfor uu in [1/2, 1, 3/2, 2, 3] do\n  print(\"  \", float(uu), \"   \", float(subst(u=uu, Kp)))$\n\n/* Solve the constant-curvature ODE directly, to show where the profile\n   comes from rather than pulling it out of a hat. */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"for a unit-speed profile K = −f''/f, so K = −1 means f'' = f:\")$\nsol : ode2( 'diff(ff,u,2) - ff = 0, ff, u )$\nprint(\"   general solution: \", sol)$\nprint(\"   f = e^(−u) with g' = √(1 − f'²) gives the tractrix profile.\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"for K = +1 the ODE is f'' = −f:\")$\nsol2 : ode2( 'diff(ff,u,2) + ff = 0, ff, u )$\nprint(\"   general solution: \", sol2, \"   — f = sin u gives the sphere\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"exponential versus trigonometric: the whole difference between\")$\nprint(\"negative and positive curvature, visible in one ODE.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 9,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "pseudosphere σ = (sech u cos v, sech u sin v, u − tanh u):\n   I =  matrix([(sinh(u)^4+sinh(u)^2)/cosh(u)^4,0],[0,1/cosh(u)^2])\n   K =  -((cosh(u)^4-cosh(u)^2)/(sinh(u)^4+sinh(u)^2))\n   after cosh²−1 = sinh² :  -1\n   after sinh²+1 = cosh² :  -1    — constant, K ≡ −1 ✓\n   u        K(u)\n   0.5     -0.9999999999999992\n   1.0     -1.0\n   1.5     -1.0\n   2.0     -1.0\n   3.0     -0.9999999999999997\nfor a unit-speed profile K = −f''/f, so K = −1 means f'' = f:\n   general solution:  ff = %e^-u*%k2+%e^u*%k1\n   f = e^(−u) with g' = √(1 − f'²) gives the tractrix profile.\nfor K = +1 the ODE is f'' = −f:\n   general solution:  ff = %k1*sin(u)+%k2*cos(u)\n                        — f = sin u gives the sphere\nexponential versus trigonometric: the whole difference between\nnegative and positive curvature, visible in one ODE."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* The generating profiles: tractrix (K = −1) against a circle (K = +1) */\nus : makelist(float(k/200), k, 1, 700)$\ntractrix : [discrete, map(lambda([t], float(sech(t))), us),\n                      map(lambda([t], float(t - tanh(t))), us)]$\nts2 : makelist(float(%pi*k/400), k, 1, 399)$\ncircle_prof : [discrete, map(lambda([t], float(sin(t))), ts2),\n                         map(lambda([t], float(1 - cos(t))), ts2)]$\n\nprint(\"tractrix profile (f, g) = (sech u, u − tanh u):\")$\nprint(\"   at u → 0⁺ : (\", float(sech(0.01)), \", \", float(0.01 - tanh(0.01)), \")\")$\nprint(\"   at u = 3  : (\", float(sech(3)),    \", \", float(3 - tanh(3)),    \")\")$\nprint(\"   f → 0 as u → ∞: the surface tapers to an infinite spike,\")$\nprint(\"   and has a cusp at u = 0 where the profile meets the axis vertically.\")$\n\nplot2d([ tractrix, circle_prof ],\n       [x, 0, 1.2], [y, 0, 3],\n       [style, [lines,3],[lines,2]],\n       [legend, \"tractrix → pseudosphere (K = −1)\", \"circle → sphere (K = +1)\"],\n       [xlabel, \"f(u) — distance from the axis of revolution\"],\n       [ylabel, \"g(u)\"],\n       [title, \"generating profiles of the two constant-curvature surfaces\"])$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 10,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "tractrix profile (f, g) = (sech u, u − tanh u):\n   at u → 0⁺ : ( 0.9999500020832486 ,  3.3332000053988564e-7 )\n   at u = 3  : ( 0.0993279274194332 ,  2.0049452463132695 )\n   f → 0 as u → ∞: the surface tapers to an infinite spike,\n   and has a cusp at u = 0 where the profile meets the axis vertically."
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "image/svg+xml": [
              "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"  standalone=\"no\"?>\n<svg \n width=\"600\" height=\"480\"\n viewBox=\"0 0 600 480\"\n xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"\n xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"\n>\n\n<title>Gnuplot</title>\n<desc>Produced by GNUPLOT 6.0 patchlevel 4 </desc>\n\n<g id=\"gnuplot_canvas\">\n\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"600\" height=\"480\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<defs>\n\n\t<circle id='gpDot' r='0.5' stroke-width='0.5' stroke='currentColor'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt0' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M-1,0 h2 M0,-1 v2'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt1' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M-1,-1 L1,1 M1,-1 L-1,1'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt2' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M-1,0 L1,0 M0,-1 L0,1 M-1,-1 L1,1 M-1,1 L1,-1'/>\n\t<rect id='gpPt3' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' x='-1' y='-1' width='2' height='2'/>\n\t<rect id='gpPt4' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' fill='currentColor' x='-1' y='-1' width='2' height='2'/>\n\t<circle id='gpPt5' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' cx='0' cy='0' r='1'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt5' id='gpPt6' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt7' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M0,-1.33 L-1.33,0.67 L1.33,0.67 z'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt7' id='gpPt8' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt7' id='gpPt9' stroke='currentColor' transform='rotate(180)'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt9' id='gpPt10' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt3' id='gpPt11' stroke='currentColor' transform='rotate(45)'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt11' id='gpPt12' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt13' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M0,1.330 L1.265,0.411 L0.782,-1.067 L-0.782,-1.076 L-1.265,0.411 z'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt13' id='gpPt14' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<filter id='textbox' filterUnits='objectBoundingBox' x='0' y='0' height='1' width='1'>\n\t  <feFlood flood-colo