    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n| Surface | $K$ | $H$ | Character |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| sphere $R$ | $1/R^2$ | $\\mp 1/R$ | elliptic, umbilic everywhere |\n| cylinder $R$ | $0$ | $-1/(2R)$ | parabolic, intrinsically flat |\n| paraboloid at $0$ | $+4$ | $\\ne 0$ | elliptic |\n| saddle at $0$ | $-4$ | $0$ | hyperbolic, and minimal at that point |\n| catenoid | $-1/\\cosh^4 u$ | $0$ | **minimal** |\n| helicoid | $-1/(1+u^2)^2$ | $0$ | **minimal** |\n| torus | changes sign | — | all three types on one surface |\n| pseudosphere | $-1$ | — | constant negative curvature |\n\nFor a surface of revolution, the general formula was derived and then collapsed, using both $f'^2 + g'^2 = 1$ and its derivative $f'f'' + g'g'' = 0$, to the classical\n\n$$K = -\\frac{f''}{f}.$$\n\nConstant curvature then becomes an ODE, and the two signs give qualitatively different answers:\n\n$$K = -1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; f'' = f \\;\\Rightarrow\\; f = e^{\\pm u} \\quad\\text{(tractrix, pseudosphere)}$$\n$$K = +1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; f'' = -f \\;\\Rightarrow\\; f = \\sin u \\quad\\text{(circle, sphere)}$$\n\n**Exponential versus trigonometric.** That single distinction is the analytic root of everything separating hyperbolic from spherical geometry — the exponential growth of area in 2.06 versus the boundedness of the sphere in 2.07, and later the divergence of geodesics under negative curvature versus their refocusing under positive curvature (4.06).\n\n## Two asymmetries worth noticing\n\n**$K$ and $H$ are not siblings.** $K$ survives orientation reversal, will be proved intrinsic in 3.06, and is topological in the aggregate (3.08). $H$ does none of these — it changes sign with the normal and depends essentially on the embedding. But $H$ has its own life: $H = 0$ is the Euler–Lagrange equation for area, which is why soap films are minimal surfaces, and 3.09 pursues that.\n\n**Positive and negative curvature are not mirror images.** The sphere realises constant $K > 0$ completely and compactly in $\\mathbb R^3$. The pseudosphere realises constant $K < 0$ only partially — it is incomplete, with a cusp — and by **Hilbert's theorem (1901)** no complete surface of constant negative curvature embeds in $\\mathbb R^3$ at all. The hyperbolic plane of 2.06 genuinely cannot be built in ordinary space, which retrospectively justifies constructing it abstractly.\n\n## Next\n\n**3.06 — Theorema Egregium** proves the claim this stage has been circling: $K$, defined here through the second fundamental form and hence through the embedding, is computable from $E, F, G$ alone. The cylinder's $K = 0$ then becomes forced rather than coincidental.\n\n## References\n\n- do Carmo, M.P. (1976) *Differential Geometry of Curves and Surfaces*. Englewood Cliffs: Prentice-Hall. §3-3 for $K$ and $H$ and the classification of points; §3-5 for surfaces of revolution; §5-11 for Hilbert's theorem.\n- Pressley, A. (2010) *Elementary Differential Geometry*. 2nd edn. Springer Undergraduate Mathematics Series. London: Springer. Chapters 8–9, and Chapter 12 on minimal surfaces.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9. Chapter 8 for the general theory, and Chapter 3 for constant-curvature model spaces.\n- Beltrami, E. (1868) 'Saggio di interpretazione della geometria non-euclidea', *Giornale di Matematiche*, 6, pp. 284–312. The paper that introduced the pseudosphere as a model of non-Euclidean geometry.\n- Hilbert, D. (1901) 'Über Flächen von constanter Gaussscher Krümmung', *Transactions of the American Mathematical Society*, 2(1), pp. 87–99. The non-embedding theorem.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}